Tag links now keep the view mode (list only, map and list, or map only). Two acceptance criteria are not met, and I did no browser check at 1440px or 390px.

The app already had a client-side router that intercepts clicks, calls `pushState`, handles `popstate`, skips modified clicks and updates the title. The real gap was that a move between list pages dropped the view mode, because each page reads its mode from `?atlas=` in the URL. `Router.navigate` in `router.ts` now adds the current `atlas` mode to `/` and `/tags/<slug>` targets, using a new helper `carryStateToPath` in `atlasLayerState.ts`. Post views and URLs that already name a mode are left alone. Back and Forward restore the mode too.

- **Not done:** if the content fetch fails, `TagPage` shows its error state. It does not fall back to a full page load.
- **Not done:** I did not build a separate router module. The existing router already does the job, so US-004 and US-008 can reuse it.
- **Map reload:** the map is rebuilt on each page change, not kept alive.
- **Checks:** the new unit test and the new e2e test pass. The e2e test opens map-only on `/`, clicks a tag link, and checks there is no reload, the mode stays `map`, and Back restores it. `scripts/check.sh` still fails on three `atlas-layer-header` e2e tests. They fail the same way when I stash my changes.

I appended the learnings to `.ralph-tui/progress.md` and closed the bead with a handoff note.

